<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic workflows promise efficiency, but adoption hinges on whether people actually trust systems that act on their behalf. We present DoubleAgents, an agentic planning tool that embeds transparency and control through user intervention, value-reflecting policies, rich state visualizations, and uncertainty flagging for human coordination tasks. A built-in respondent simulation generates realistic scenarios, allowing users to rehearse, refine policies, and calibrate their reliance before live use. We evaluate DoubleAgents in a two-day lab study (n=10), two deployments (n=2), and a technical evaluation. Results show that participants initially hesitated to delegate but grew more reliant as they experienced transparency, control, and adaptive learning during simulated cases. Deployment results demonstrate DoubleAgents’ real-world relevance and usefulness, showing that the effort required scaled appropriately with task complexity and contextual data. We contribute trust-by-design patterns and mechanisms for proactive AI—consistency, controllability, and explainability—along with simulation as a safe path to build and calibrate trust over time.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>generative AI, AI agents, trust, reliance, transparency, state tracking, seminar organizing, control, planning, human coordination, reasoning, constitutional AI
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Coordinating human effort is essential for tasks at every scale. Event planning involves finding suitable times, locations, vendors, and transportation. Warehouses must assign workers with the right skills and experience to shifts they are available for. Even organizing a speaker series requires emailing potential speakers, collecting their availability, and assigning them to appropriate slots. These problems involve both discovering people’s availability and solving the constraints of assigning individuals to limited roles. Human coordinators face time-consuming and intricate communication challenges: people may hesitate to disclose their full availability, delay responses, or provide only tentative answers such as “I might be able to come Saturday, but I’m not sure yet.” Coordinators must also balance group dynamics, make exceptions for important individuals, and continuously replan when last-minute changes arise. Coordination is thus both tedious and delicate, requiring continuous monitoring and re-planning while respecting social norms and relationships.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Generative AI now possesses several key capabilities that could help with coordination, including deep reasoning to evaluate constraints and produce plans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stechly et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and API tool-calling to take concrete actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masterman et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, people do not yet trust AI to act proactively on their behalf for complex and delicate tasks like human coordination problems. AI systems are prone to hallucinations, often lack sufficient context to understand the problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Athaluri et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and may mishandle delicate social interactions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Most importantly, they lack an awareness of when to stop and escalate to human oversight. As a result, current AI interfaces largely wait for users to issue prompts and micromanage responses. This limits productivity and prevents AI from relieving people of burdensome procedural work. To unlock proactive AI, we need frameworks that let humans observe, guide, and calibrate trust in AI over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Trust is not established by statements alone—it develops through repeated reliable behavior over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Razin and Feigh, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fouragnan et al., 2013</a>)</cite>. Game theory suggests that trust between humans is built by repeated observation of behavior in varied circumstances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kollock, 1998</a>)</cite>. Additionally, in the AI trust and safety literature, Constitutional AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is a paradigm that uses human-articulated policies (called a constitution) that can align an AI’s behavior with human expectations and values. Policies in making expectations explicit - so that humans and AI have a shared understanding of the expected behavior. Repeated interactions and clear policies are two pillars of establishing trust that we build upon to help build trust in proactive AI.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/system_diagram.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/510;" width="429" height="510" alt="Diagram showing the architecture of the DoubleAgents system, with components for goal setting, agent coordination, context management (including edge case detection), and various submodules such as toolbox, orchestrator, and simulated respondents.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">System diagram of DoubleAgents illustrating a day-by-day ReAct workflow that couples policy-guided planning with human oversight and LLM simulation. <span id="S1.F1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs</span> (top left) include the organizer’s goal, seminar slots, speaker personas, and a toolbox of callable functions. The <span id="S1.F1.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">coordination agent</span> (center) iteratively: summarizes state; selects applicable policies; proposes a plan and action that the user can Regenerate or Approve; executes by drafting and sending emails, or waiting for responses. If a reply falls outside policy coverage, issueFlag escalates an edge case to the user for clarification before proceeding. The <span id="S1.F1.8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">context management</span> (right) maintains time information, past plans/actions, email logs, user preferences, and persona data, continuously updating to ground subsequent steps. <span id="S1.F1.8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulated respondent agents</span> (bottom right) generate realistic, persona-consistent behaviors and replies that drive the loop across days. For a detailed walkthrough, please refer to Section 4.
</span><span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Diagram showing the architecture of the DoubleAgents system, with components for goal setting, agent coordination, context management (including edge case detection), and various submodules such as toolbox, orchestrator, and simulated respondents.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We propose to build trust in proactive AI through simulation and policy refinement. Human behavior can be unpredictable, but AI-driven simulations can generate a wide range of realistic scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, including late, incomplete, or uncertain replies. These allow coordinators to test and refine policies in safe environments. Policies specify how the AI should act (e.g., when to follow up, when to stop, how to phrase outreach) and when to escalate to human oversight. Hence, we propose to use simulation to expose AI behavior across repeated interactions, letting users correct and evolve policies iteratively. Visualizations of system state—goal progress, a timeline view, and person-level interaction histories—provide transparency and help humans intervene efficiently when needed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We present DoubleAgents, shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an interactive agentic system designed to automate aspects of human coordination while supporting user oversight and trust-building. DoubleAgents integrates generative AI agents in two complementary roles: (1) a ReAct Loop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> with AI planning and execution agents that can call tools to get appropriate context, query the current state of the system, and execute actions such as emailing potential speakers. This loop is extended to provide interaction for human interactivity and oversight at every step. (2) A LLM-based simulation of respondents that generate the varied and realistic human responses for various respondent personas (e.g. a professor who is very slow to respond, or a speaker who won’t commit). The LLM simulations allow users to experience repeated execution of the system to understand how it works, evolve the policies over time to suit their preferences, and potentially build trust that the system will act according to their wishes. By combining policy generation and refinement, action execution, and multi-agent LLM simulation within a user-centered feedback process, DoubleAgents helps users delegate repetitive aspects of coordination while retaining control of high-stakes interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In a lab study (n=10) of people using the system with simulated responses over two study sessions, participants began hesitant to delegate but, through visible state, explicit reasoning based from policies, strong intervention points, and rehearsal on realistic cases, grew more comfortable relying on the agent for both planning and execution. In two real-world deployments of the system, we found that organizers adopted DoubleAgents as transparency, policy controls, and edge<span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>case flagging built trust; however, required more upfront context setup and tighter email integration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our work demonstrates how agentic systems can support reliable, human-centered coordination by embedding simulation, transparency, and interactive oversight into the agentic workflow. Specifically, we contribute:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A policy-driven, intervention<span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>friendly approach to govern proactive AI behavior in an agentic and integactive ReAct framework to build trust with users.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An AI-driven simulation framework of human responses to support repeated observations of reliable behavior over time to test and tune policies for AI to follow.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A user study (n=10) and a deployment with two users to evaluate how users perceive and gradually trust proactive AI for a human coordination task of scheduling a speaker seminar.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Agentic AI in Task Planning and Execution</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">“AI Agents” typically refers to AI that can perform tasks for people. This can be done through tool use (namely, calling an API) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, or operating a computer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Manus AI, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labs, 2025</a>)</cite>. Although AI Agents show impressive potential to act, trusting agents to act on behalf of a human is a critical missing piece <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Afroogh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The delegation of control to AI raises questions not only about competence, but also about alignment, transparency, and user oversight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Planning and execution remain central yet unresolved challenges in the development of AI agents. A growing body of work highlights both the difficulty and the importance of task planning, as seen in benchmarks and simulation environments such as Tau-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, NaturalPlan <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, TravelPlanner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and AppWorld <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These studies underscore that robust, goal-directed planning, particularly in open-ended or multi-step domains, is not only technically demanding but also essential for delivering meaningful user value.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this challenge, recent work has explored how LLMs can scaffold user planning by decomposing high-level goals into actionable steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Techniques such as ReAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, which interleave reasoning and acting, have gained traction in multi-step environments for improving agent adaptability and responsiveness. These approaches are often embedded within multi-agent systems to support domains like travel, scheduling, and personal task management <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lal et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, autonomous systems such as AutoGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Significant Gravitas, [n. d.]</a>)</cite> often fall short in transparency, adaptability, and robustness, limiting their usability in real-world settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, prior cognitive science research shows that humans rarely engage in exhaustive upfront planning due to cognitive load <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krieger et al., 2009</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Teevan et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, highlighting the need for AI systems that can support incremental, iterative, and contextualized planning over time. Additionally, recent studies suggest that human–AI co-planning, particularly when agents expose their intermediate reasoning and allow users to guide or intervene, can improve both task performance and user trust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. When users can monitor and influence agent decisions at each step, they are better able to understand, calibrate, and ultimately trust the system, especially in domains requiring high-stakes delegation or multi-party coordination.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Human Simulations with LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has explored the use of LLMs in multi-agent simulations  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, revealing their surprising capacity to model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horton, 2023</a>)</cite>, predict, and even exhibit human-like behavior - including taking actions they were not explicitly instructed to perform in order to achieve high-level goals such as forming routines, organizing events, and collaborating spontaneously <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These behaviors often mirror human reasoning and coordination strategies.
Other studies have shown that these behaviors closely align with empirical observations of humans across various domains, including economic games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sreedhar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sreedhar and Chilton, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aher et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horton, 2023</a>)</cite>, U.S. Supreme Court judgments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hamilton, 2023</a>)</cite>, and over 80% of published and unpublished psychology experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hewitt et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These results suggest that LLM agents can serve as realistic proxies for humans in testing coordination, negotiation, and reasoning behaviors at scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">AI systems that silate human behavior have already shown a striking capability to engage in complex, social behaviors competitively well with humans, for real world purposes. .
Beyond simulation, AI systems like CICERO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2022</a>)</cite> have shown how language agents can engage directly with humans in dynamic, multi-party settings. In online Diplomacy games, CICERO achieved a top 10% ranking among human players - illustrating the potential for LLM-based systems to engage in trust-sensitive planning, social reasoning, and strategic communication. The MATRIX framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> improved alignment in constitutional AI evaluations by useing simulated AI dialogues to anticipate a range of perspectives and surface important value trade-offs.
Many systems have successfully used LLM red teaming  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Purpura et al., 2025</a>)</cite> to identify potential threats in systems.
Although AI simulations might not perfectly replicate individual user behaviors (yet) - they can be used to explore edge cases, probe systems, and engage in sensitive dialogs with humans.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Constitutional AI</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Constitutional AI is a framework for aligning AI systems with human values by training models to self-critique and revise their outputs based on a set of guiding principles, or a "constitution"<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Unlike reinforcement learning with human feedback (RLHF)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which requires human evaluation for each model response, Constitutional AI allows the model to evaluate its own behavior using pre-defined rules. These constitutions are typically expressed as human-readable policies such as “Don’t promote illegal activity” or “Be helpful and respectful,” and can be authored by anyone. Building on this idea, Collective Constitutional AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduced a participatory approach in which over 1,000 people contributed rules to a shared constitution. Models trained on this crowd-sourced constitution demonstrated qualitatively different behaviors compared to those trained on developer-authored rules—highlighting the diversity of human values and community norms, and the implications this has for model alignment. To create constitutions, interactive systems like ConstitutionMaker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petridis et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have shown that LLMs can help transform users’ natural language feedback into generalized alignment policies. By providing real-time AI assistance in policy creation, such systems help users articulate their values more effectively, reduce cognitive burden, and make the process of shaping AI behavior more accessible and inclusive. These advances open up opportunities for systems that incorporate user-driven policy creation and alignment into workflows that have real-world impacts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Seminar Planning: A Human Coordination Problem</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human coordination—such as assigning work shifts or scheduling job interviews—is both cognitively demanding and socially delicate. Unlike tasks with stable inputs and predictable outcomes, coordination requires managing uncertainty, reconciling multiple perspectives, and sustaining relationships over time. Even small lapses—missed follow-ups, unclear tone, or misaligned expectations—can erode trust and derail collective progress. These challenges make coordination both essential and persistently difficult in many areas of knowledge work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We study these challenges through one concrete and consequential setting: organizing an academic seminar series. A typical series spans an entire semester and involves inviting a new external speaker nearly every week, amounting to 8–12 speakers per term. Early on, organizers must identify potential speakers, gather their availability constraints, and balance their own, such as ensuring topical diversity across weeks and securing highly sought-after senior researchers who are harder to schedule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the most unpredictable and challenging part is dealing with responses. Some people do not reply at all and require multiple follow-ups to elicit a response. Some give only vague and tentative replies that need repeated confirmation. Some can be flakey and unreliable, offering slots but later canceling. And many unexpected problem cases arise: people might be on parental leave, ask for visa letters or funding support, recommend a different speaker, request a different date or talk modality, or raise logistical demands. In such human coordination tasks, you can rarely predict what is coming. Therefore, conventional scheduling tools such as Calendly<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          https://calendly.com/</span></span></span> or When2Meet<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          https://www.when2meet.com/</span></span></span> are ill-suited to this setting. They assume stable constraints and one-shot coordination, yet seminar scheduling requires ongoing negotiation, persuasion, and relationship maintenance. Unlike ordinary meeting scheduling, seminar speakers are doing you a favor, which means you cannot simply send them a link to pick a time—you must persuade and negotiate. Social dynamics and etiquette also play a large role: organizers must time their emails carefully to avoid overwhelming, annoying, or losing momentum with potential speakers, who are often busy scholars. Knowing when to nudge, follow up, or wait is subtle. Email remains the standard medium for professional and academic outreach, allowing organizers to adapt tone—formal, casual, or enthusiastic—to different speakers in ways scheduling links cannot. It also supports sharing rich context, such as talk topics, background, past speakers, or travel plans—far beyond simply choosing a calendar slot.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, experienced organizers adopt personal strategies to cope with such a demanding task. For example, some follow a rule of “wait two days, then follow up” to nudge busy people who may not read emails right away, while others wait a full week before reaching out again. Some prefer contacting close collaborators or junior researchers first, while others prioritize securing prominent professors or distant speakers early to reduce travel constraints. When conflicts arise between speakers, organizers might “give faculty priority and ask students to be more flexible,” carefully deciding whom to approach for alternative availability to minimize disruption. For non-responders or last-minute cancellations, they must judge when and how often to follow up before moving on to new options—and sometimes even decide when it is appropriate to “beg” busy invitees for additional availability. Because plans are constantly evolving, organizers become locked in a cognitively and time-intensive loop of tracking shifting states, replanning, and executing revised plans as new information arrives—yet they often cannot proceed with the next steps or finalize allocations until replies are received.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since seminar scheduling requires nuanced, context-dependent decisions that reflect each organizer’s values and preferences, it is difficult to fully automate. An effective seminar scheduling tool should not attempt to handle the process independently; instead, it must learn from and adapt to the organizer, respecting social norms, relational dynamics, and the inherent unpredictability of responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>DoubleAgents System</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/screenshot-all.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/277;" width="476" height="277" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.F2.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A screenshot of DoubleAgents coordinating the assignment of four speakers to four seminar slots. <span id="S4.F2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A) Policy Panel</span> – Displays coordination policies that reflect user values and preferences. Users can add, edit, or delete policies, which guide the reasoning and planning of the coordination agent.
<span id="S4.F2.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B) Interactive Chat Interface</span> – Shows the full history of plans and actions, allowing users to monitor planning and execution, confirm or modify agent suggestions, and intervene when necessary. <span id="S4.F2.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C) Plan and Action Pop-up</span> – A focused view within the chat history where users can review, edit, regenerate, or approve individual plans and actions. <span id="S4.F2.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D) Assignment Tracker</span> – Visualizes tentative speaker-to-slot assignments and indicates each speaker’s availability. Dark purple denotes confirmed availability; light purple signals ambiguous availability requiring user confirmation. <span id="S4.F2.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(E) Communication History Viewer </span>– Displays the messaging history between the coordination agent and each speaker, organized by date and interaction. <span id="S4.F2.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(F) Calendar View</span> – Provides temporal context by showing the current simulation and upcoming seminar dates, color-coded for clarity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present DoubleAgents, an interactive agentic system designed to investigate how users build and calibrate trust in proactive AI systems. Built on the ReAct framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>, DoubleAgents enables iterative planning and execution through a team of AI agents that assist users in complex coordination tasks. In this work, we focus on the use case of assigning seminar speakers to available time slots. The system takes as input a user-defined goal (e.g., “Organize a seminar with four speakers and four slots.”) and produces as output a finalized assignment of speakers to slots. DoubleAgents consists of six core components, grouped into the following three functional categories. First, it contains the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Core ReAct Agents</span> that carry out the reasoning, planning, and simulation logic that drive the coordination process:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coordination Agent</span> - Responsible for reasoning over the current problem state using a set of predefined policies. It proposes plans and actions, and invokes appropriate tools to execute them.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulated Respondents</span> - Represent background agents that emulate human respondents’ behavior by responding to user-generated emails based on predefined personas and descriptions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Second, DoubleAgents incorporates the <span id="S4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Orchestration Modules</span>, which manage the internal context and ensure robustness during decision-making:</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Context Management Module</span> - Maintains all relevant contextual information related to the task, including simulated respondents’ details (e.g., personas and descriptions), email logs from prior time steps, and the history of user-approved plans, actions, and emails.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge Case Detector</span> - Identifies situations that fall outside the coverage of existing policies and escalates them to the user for clarification and additional instructions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Lastly, the <span id="S4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">User Interaction Interfaces</span> components enable transparency, user control, and real-time feedback during the coordination process:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interactive Chat Interface</span> - Facilitates communication between the user and the system. Users can confirm or revise generated plans, actions, and emails, and respond to flagged edge cases by providing natural language feedback.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualization Dashboard</span> - Provides real-time feedback on the state of the task, including temporal progress, assignment status, and an activity tracker to help users monitor the overall coordination process.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide a walkthrough example to illustrate how DoubleAgents operates in practice. We also describe the implementation details of DoubleAgents. Consider the example of Lee, a PhD student organizing the HCI seminar series at his university. Lee plans to invite four speakers–Prof. A (a famous professor), Prof. B (a rising star professor), Student X (a senior PhD student), and Student Y (a PhD student who is a friend of Lee) – to deliver talks during four available time slots: Sep 5, Oct 4, Nov 7, and Dec 5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Lee begins at the landing page, where he specifies his goals to the system. He types: “I am trying to organize a seminar with four speakers and four slots, help me reach out”. He then provides additional details, including the seminar location, and the dates of the four time slots. Next, Lee enters persona information for each speaker he wants to invite, which the system uses to generate simulated speaker profiles. The landing page is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. To further customize communication, Lee uploads five of his past emails, allowing the system to adapt its generated messages to his style; for instance, casual for friends and formal for faculty. Finally, Lee initiates the simulation by clicking the “Start Planning” button, prompting the system to set up the scenario with the specified speakers and time slots. At this point, he is also shown a list of preset policies (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(A)), which serve as guidelines for the coordination agent to begin planning. Lee reviews the policies, all of which appear reasonable. He may modify existing policies or introduce new ones to better guide the agent’s planning process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/landing_1.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/475;" width="476" height="475" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/landing_2.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/525;" width="476" height="525" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The landing page to specify the user goals, seminar details, and the persona info for the speakers.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Context Management Module</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of the scheduling process, all information provided by Lee on the landing page is compiled into a structured problem context that the system consults at each step to solve the assignment task. DoubleAgents represents this problem context using JSON structures, where each key denotes a specific type of contextual information (e.g., speaker profiles, time constraints, email history, etc.), and each value contains the corresponding content. As shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, an example snapshot of the problem context includes keys such as "time info", which reflects the seminar slots specified by Lee. The context is dynamically updated throughout the interaction, incorporating user feedback, agent actions, and simulated responses to maintain an up-to-date representation of the task state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Interactive Chat Interface</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Upon clicking “Start Planning,” Lee is directed to the main Interactive Chat Interface (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(B)), where he can view the full history of system interactions and monitor the planning and execution progress carried out by the core ReAct agents. All plans, actions, and emails generated by the system are accessible through this interface. Each system update appears as an interactive pop-up window (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(C) shows a pop-up plan item), enabling Lee to inspect and confirm the system output in a controllable and traceable way. The interface also supports natural language queries, giving Lee full control over what to view. For example, he can type “<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">What did Prof. B reply in the last email?</span>” to retrieve the relevant message history directly within the chat. This chat functionality is powered by GPT-4o, with prompt details provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4. Free-form QA Chat ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Coordination Agent</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1. </span>Progress Understanding</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When Lee initiates the planning process, the coordination agent assesses the overall state of the problem by generating a concise summary of the progress so far. To do that, it first reviews the current state of the scheduling process by analyzing the problem context, including speakers’ info, email logs from previous days, and the history of plans and actions previously approved by Lee. Based on this information, the coordination agent generates a summary of the current status, indicating which days have been scheduled, what actions have been taken, and the most recent contact date for each speaker to help monitor responses and follow-ups. The prompt used in generating the summary is illustrated in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS1" title="A.1.1. Progress Summary ‣ A.1. Coordination Agent ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2. </span>Policy Selection</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To design a set of general policies applicable to a broad range of planning problems, five experts were recruited to brainstorm and define a framework of rules tailored to address common scheduling challenges. These challenges include: (1) individuals being reluctant to share their full availability, (2) ambiguous or unclear responses, (3) the need for multiple follow-ups to elicit responses, (4) resolving scheduling conflicts between two speakers, and (5) initiating the process by broadly reaching out to potential participants. Based on these identified challenges, we developed six initial "seed policies" as a core set of guidelines for the system to reference and select from when generating plans. Each policy is designed to address a specific challenge, with two policies dedicated to handling non-responsive participants: one focused on strategies to cope with the lack of response (follow-up frequency) and another providing a framework for when to give up on follow-ups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Lee’s example, since it is the first day of scheduling, the progress summary informs the system that no speaker availabilities have been collected yet. As a result, the coordination agent invokes the policy selection tool, which identifies the appropriate policies based on the current problem state. Drawing from the list of "seed policies", the system selects: “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If we are just beginning and have not yet contacted any speakers for availability, then ask each speaker for their availability across all slots.</span>” The selected policy is then highlighted in the Policy Panel (as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(A)), providing Lee with the rationale to generate the plan. The policy acts as the planning guideline, informing the agent’s next steps. Details of the prompts used for the policy selection module are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS2" title="A.1.2. Policy Selection ‣ A.1. Coordination Agent ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/plan_gen.png" id="S4.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/407;" width="476" height="407" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The pop-up window for a generated plan.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/action_gen.png" id="S4.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/747;" width="476" height="747" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The pop-up window for generated actions following the plan.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F4.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/email.png" id="S4.F4.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/590;" width="476" height="590" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F4.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The pop-up window for a generated email.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Examples for plan and action generation.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3. </span>Plan Generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Guided by this policy, the coordination agent generates the following plan (shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.2. Policy Selection ‣ 4.3. Coordination Agent ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>) for Lee: “Send email to Prof. A, Prof. B, Student X, and Student Y to request availability for slots on Sep 5, Oct 4, Nov 7, and Dec 5.”. Lee considers the plan and the rationale logical, since scheduling cannot proceed without first collecting availability information, and clicks ’Approve &amp; Continue’ to move forward. The plan and its rationale are automatically recorded in the interactive chat interface (shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(B)), allowing Lee to review past activities and understand the rationale behind the plan. If Lee is dissatisfied with the proposed plan, he can either request the system to regenerate an alternative or directly edit the current plan himself, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.2. Policy Selection ‣ 4.3. Coordination Agent ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.4. </span>Action Generation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To operationalize the plan, the coordination agent then generates a set of concrete actions to be executed (see Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.2. Policy Selection ‣ 4.3. Coordination Agent ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>). Specifically, it proposes sending individual emails to each speaker, with the stated goal: “Request for availability for Seminar Slots (Sep 5, Oct 3, Nov 7, Dec 5).” These proposed actions are presented to Lee for review. He examines each action and its corresponding goal, deems them appropriate, and clicks Approve to proceed. Importantly, Lee retains full control over the suggested actions: he may edit the goals, remove actions that are misaligned, or instruct the system to regenerate the set based on revised input. In general, there are three types of actions: (1) drafting emails to selected respondents, (2) sending emails to specified recipients using their provided addresses, and (3) awaiting responses from certain respondents. The detailed prompts used for plan and action generation are provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS3" title="A.1.3. Action Generation ‣ A.1. Coordination Agent ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.5. </span>Action Execution</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following approval, the coordination agent proceeds to instantiate each action by generating email drafts for the designated speakers, including both subject lines and message bodies (Figure <a href="#S4.F4.sf3" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 4.3.2. Policy Selection ‣ 4.3. Coordination Agent ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(c)</span></a>). This is done by calling the email writing tool, as specified in the tool box shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Specifically, it calls the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">writeEmail()</span> API, which drafts emails given the current problem state, seminar details, user-provided templates, and historical email edits. These elements allow the system to learn and adapt to the Lee’s preferred communication style, capturing tone, level of formality, and domain-specific phrasing, when generating new emails. Note that email drafting is performed using GPT<span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>4o, which is invoked by the coordination agent during the execution of the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">writeEmail()</span> tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lee reviews each draft and identifies one instance where the tone is not appropriate—the email to Prof. A, a distinguished professor, feels overly informal. To address this, Lee types: “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Please make the email sound more formal, as Prof. A is a distinguished professor I’d like to invite.</span>” The system then regenerates a more suitable version. Lee further refines the message by editing the first paragraph to better reflect his personal voice: “<span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">My name is Lee, a PhD student in Computer Science. Your work in [XXX] has greatly inspired us, and I would be honored to invite you to speak at our HCI seminar series.</span>” After reviewing and finalizing all drafts, Lee is satisfied and clicks ‘Approve &amp; Send’, prompting the system to dispatch the emails to all invited speakers. This is accomplished by invoking the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sendEmail()</span> API by the coordination agent, targeting the appropriate speaker email addresses specified in the problem context. In addition, all email-related activities are logged in the dashboard (Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(E)), enabling Lee to easily monitor the status and history of interactions with each speaker. Besides writing and sending emails, the coordination agent may invoke actions involving delayed responses, such as waiting for a speaker’s reply. This is achieved by calling the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">waitResponse()</span> tool. In such cases, no message is generated or sent; instead, the agent pauses execution and either proceeds to the next pending action or advances to the next simulated day. Overall, we use GPT-o3 as the backbone of the coordination agent, given its strong reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.6. </span>Implicit User Preference Learning</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DoubleAgents supports implicit learning of user preferences by observing user-initiated edits to system-generated plans, actions, or emails. For instance, if the coordination agent proposes to reach out to Student X to ask for more availability, Lee may choose to remove the action <span id="S4.SS3.SSS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Send initial email to Student X”</span>—perhaps because Student X’s prior responses have been vague or unhelpful. The system treats such edits as preference signals. To act on these signals, the coordination agent summarizes the edit into a concise statement, logs it in a user edit history, and incorporates it into the problem context. This updated context is then referenced in subsequent planning steps, enabling the agent to evolve its behavior in alignment with the user’s preferences over time. To streamline the summarization process, we leverage GPT-4o. Details of the prompt used for generating summaries of user edits across various content types can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS1.SSS5" title="A.1.5. Implicit User Preference Learning ‣ A.1. Coordination Agent ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1.5</span></a>. The user-initiated edits, the corresponding system-generated drafts, and the generated summaries of the edits are all preserved in the user edit history. This comprehensive history serves as an evolving knowledge base, directly influencing the system’s generation of future plans, actions, and emails, and ensuring that it remains responsive to the nuances of user behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Simulated Respondent Agent</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To construct a realistic simulation environment for Lee, we draw inspiration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and employ LLMs to simulate each respondent agent, generating actions that align with their assigned personas. Concretely, upon receiving Lee’s emails, each simulated respondent agent first reasons over its persona and then generates an action that adheres strictly to those characteristics. For instance, the agent representing Prof. A opts not to respond on the same day (shown in Figure <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), as his persona specifies: “<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">You are very busy and reply very late. Normally, it takes you three follow-up emails to respond.</span>” In contrast, Prof. B’s persona indicates she is prompt in communication, leading her agent to reply immediately. Given that Prof.B’s persona also specifies preferences, such as “prefers presenting over Zoom”, the generated action will explicitly request a Zoom presentation option. The prompt for speaker agent, both for generating actions and drafting email responses, is detailed in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3. Simulated Respondents ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>. To execute the selected actions, the simulated respondent agents compose and send email responses, which are then received by Lee on his end. Figure <a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 4.4. Simulated Respondent Agent ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> shows an example response received by Lee as shown in the interface. These responses advance the overall scheduling process and enable Lee to continue coordinating subsequent steps. Similar to the coordination agent, we adopt GPT-o3 as the backbone of each respondent agent to perform reasoning and generate plans and actions. GPT-4o is used when invoking the <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sendEmail()</span> tool to generate email responses.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/email_receive_profA.png" id="S4.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/333;" width="476" height="333" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Email sent by the simulated Prof. A.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/email_receive_profB_flag.png" id="S4.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/589;" width="476" height="589" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F5.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Edge case detection given response from Prof. B asking about Zoom options.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The example email sent by the simulated respondents and the system message when detecting edge case.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5. </span>Edge Case Detector</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Within a day, Lee receives an email from Prof. B stating: “<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thanks for reaching out, Lee! I was wondering if there might be an option to present via Zoom instead of in person? That would provide more flexibility for me.</span>” As this type of request had not been previously addressed by any existing policy, representing an edge case, the edge case detector issues a flag to prompt clarification from Lee. Through the Interactive Chat Interface, Lee sees the following system message: “<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prof. B requests a Zoom option due to circumstances preventing in-person attendance. Should I confirm Zoom availability for their seminar or insist on in-person participation?</span>” Preferring in-person interactions for seminars, Lee responds with the instruction: “<span id="S4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Tell Prof. B we prefer in-person due to the potentially large audience.</span>” The coordination agent then processes this clarification and generates a new action to inform Prof. B accordingly. Lee reviews the suggested action, finds it appropriate, and clicks Approve. An email draft is subsequently generated; after confirming the message aligns with his intent, Lee clicks ’Approve &amp; Send’ to dispatch the response to Prof. B.
In general, the edge case detector acts as a tool for the coordination agent to check if the message sent from the respondent agent can be addressed with the existing set of policies in order to make the next plans. When the detector identifies an edge case, the coordination agent escalates the issue to the user by presenting a clarification question in the interactive chat interface, prompting the user to provide guidance on how to proceed. The edge case detector is implemented with GPT-4o (see prompts in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2. Edge Case Detection ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6. </span>Dashboard Visualization</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To support user understanding and foster trust, <span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DoubleAgents</span> provides clear visual feedback on the task state and system progress throughout the scheduling process. We implement a Flask-based dashboard interface designed to make the planning workflow interactive, legible, and actionable. The dashboard is composed of several layered visual components, shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(D), (E), and (F), which we describe in detail below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assignment Grid and Progress Tracker</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>D): This panel visualizes tentative and confirmed speaker-to-slot assignments. It highlights which seminar slots remain unfilled and which speakers have been successfully scheduled. This layer helps Lee assess how close the current plan is to completion at a glance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Calendar and Timelines</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>F): Anchored in simulated days, this temporal layer helps Lee reason about timing, deadlines, and follow-up actions. The calendar highlights both the current simulated day and the seminar dates, allowing Lee to prioritize actions based on urgency and scheduling constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Communication Visualization</span> (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>E): This panel displays the message history between the coordination agent and each speaker, offering a transparent view of the outreach and response status. It enables Lee to track ongoing communications, detect gaps, and assess follow-up needs.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These visual layers operate in parallel, allowing the system to efficiently track and update the state of each element in real time. Throughout the process, Lee interacts with the coordination agent and the respondent agents to collaboratively establish speaker-to-slot assignments for the seminar. Together, these visualization features provide Lee with a clear overview of assignment progress and a sense of what needs to be done on the current and subsequent days.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At the end of each simulated day, the system generates a daily summary that highlights key events and decisions. Specifically, we use GPT-4o to generate the daily summaries, with prompt details provided in the Appendix <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5. Daily Summary ‣ Appendix A DoubleAgents Prompts ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. DoubleAgents System ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(B), one example summary is presented within the Interactive Chat Interface and reads: “<span id="S4.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Invitations for the HCI seminar were sent to Profs. A, B, and Students X, Y. Prof. B and Student Y expressed interest, with scheduling pending. Both Prof. B and Student X inquired about Zoom options instead of in-person attendance.</span>” After reviewing the summary, Lee confirms the day’s progress and clicks Continue to Next Day to proceed. This iterative, day-by-day process continues until all seminar slots are successfully assigned.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Technical Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DoubleAgents enables users to engage in iterative task planning and action execution through two core functionalities: (1) policy selection, which guides the generation of plans aligned with predefined coordination strategies; and (2) robust edge case detection, which identifies problematic or ambiguous situations and escalates them to the user for clarification and resolution. In this section, we describe the technical evaluation of the two components.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Policy Selection Experiments</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Experiment settings</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Policy selection refers to the process of identifying relevant coordination policies that guide the generation of high-quality, context-appropriate plans. Specifically, given a particular problem state (e.g., unfilled time slots and unresponsive speakers), the policy selection module is expected to retrieve all applicable policies that can serve as guidelines for the next planning step. For example, if no speakers have been contacted yet, a suitable policy would be: “<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If we are just beginning and have not yet contacted any speakers for availability, then ask each speaker for their availability across all slots.</span>” In our implementation, we adopt a seed set of general-purpose coordination policies curated by domain experts. To inform policy selection, we use the output of the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">getProgressSummary()</span> API, which provides a high-level snapshot of the current task state. An example summary reads: “<span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One slot has been secured: speaker 4 confirmed for Nov 7 (2025-11-07) and received a confirmation email on 2025-08-07. All other speakers are still pending. Last outreach dates: speaker 1 and speaker 2 were last emailed on 2025-08-08; speaker 3 was last emailed on 2025-08-07.</span>”</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated the effectiveness of our approach by comparing it against three baseline variants, each using a reduced context: (1) speaker-only context, which includes only information about speaker responsiveness; (2) slot-only context, which includes only slot-level availability (e.g., filled vs. unfilled); and (3) combined speaker and slot context, which merges the two but omits higher-level summaries. Prompt templates for all conditions are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Technical Evaluation Details ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. To evaluate the performance of each approach, we constructed a test suite of 30 examples, all sourced from real user study sessions and curated to reflect a diverse set of scheduling scenarios. For each example, we treat the set of policies approved by users during the study as the ground truth for evaluation. All evaluations were conducted using the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o4-mini</span> model with a default temperature of 1. We report the average F1 score across three independent runs for each experimental setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Results and Findings</h4>

<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Prompting</span></span>
<span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T1.3.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Techniques</span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">F1 Score</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Mean</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">SD</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Speaker-only</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.27</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.054</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Slot-only</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">.11</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">.038</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Slot + Speaker</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">.27</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">.017</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Summary-based</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T1.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.70</span>***</td>
<td id="S5.T1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">.035</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The technical evaluation results for Policy Section comparing four prompting techniques. The best scores are shown in bold. Statistically significant improvements (independent two-sample t-test, <math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) over all baselines are marked with ***.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As summarized in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1.2. Results and Findings ‣ 5.1. Policy Selection Experiments ‣ 5. Technical Evaluation ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, our summary-based context approach achieves the highest F1 score for predicting relevant policies given a specific problem state. The improvement is statistically significant when compared to all three baseline conditions, with an average F1 score of 0.70, demonstrating the effectiveness of using structured progress summaries to guide policy selection. Notably, simply aggregating speaker- and slot-related context without summarization does not improve performance; the combined-context baseline yields only 0.27 average F1, indicating that raw context alone is insufficient for accurate policy retrieval. Based on these results, we adopt the summary-based approach as the default mechanism for selecting policies, which are then passed to the orchestrator agent to guide plan generation in a policy-aligned manner.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Edge Case Detection Experiments</h3>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1. </span>Experiment settings</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Edge case detection involves identifying whether a respondent’s message presents a scenario that falls outside the coverage of existing coordination policies. For example, a speaker requesting to present via Zoom, when no policy has addressed virtual presentations, should be flagged to the user for clarification and further instruction. To enable reliable detection of such edge cases, we adopt a few-shot prompting strategy with access to existing policies (denoted as "few-shot w/ policy"). We compare this approach against two baselines: (1) zero-shot prompting without any demonstration examples, and (2) few-shot prompting without access to policy references (denoted as "few-shot w/o policy"). Concrete prompt templates for each setup are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2. Edge Case Detection ‣ Appendix B Technical Evaluation Details ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. To evaluate performance, we constructed a test suite of 100 examples, manually validated by the lead authors to ensure coverage and quality. The construction methodology for this test suite is detailed in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2. Edge Case Detection ‣ Appendix B Technical Evaluation Details ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. All experiments were conducted using <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4o</span>, with temperature set to 0.7 and a maximum token limit of 100. We report the average accuracy across three independent runs for each condition.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2. </span>Results and Findings</h4>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S5.T2.3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left">Prompting Techniques</span></span>
</span></span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">Accuracy</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Mean</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">SD</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Zero-shot</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.79</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.021</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Few-shot w/o Policy</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.88†</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">.000</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Few-shot w Policy</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T2.3.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.93</span>**</td>
<td id="S5.T2.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">.015</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The technical evaluation results for Edge Case Detection comparing different prompting techniques. The best scores are shown in bold. Statistically significant improvements (independent two-sample t-test, p &lt; 0.01) over both baselines are marked with **, improvements over one baseline are marked with †.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2.2. Results and Findings ‣ 5.2. Edge Case Detection Experiments ‣ 5. Technical Evaluation ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our few-shot prompting with policy method significantly outperforms both baselines, with statistically significant improvements across evaluation runs. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of incorporating policy context into the detection process, enabling the model to first analyze existing policies before determining whether a given case falls outside their scope. Based on these findings, DoubleAgents adopts the policy-aware few-shot approach as its default mechanism for edge case detection. This ensures that novel or ambiguous cases are reliably flagged to users, enabling timely clarification and maintaining a high-quality user experience.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>User Study</h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Goal and Setup</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the user-perceived effectiveness of DoubleAgents’ features and the dynamics of trust in human-AI collaboration, we recruited 10 participants to use DoubleAgents across two separate sessions. Our goal was to assess users’ perceived system usefulness, their reliance on the systems, and their trust and comfort in using AI to automate tasks had shifted. During each session, participants worked in a simulated environment to schedule a seminar with four dates and four speakers, while the system gradually adapted to their preferences. Participants completed a questionnaire assessing their trust in 15 different system features at three points: before the first session, after the first session, and after the second session. This allowed us to examine whether and when their trust and perceptions of the system changed over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Participants</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recruited 10 participants (7 female, 3 male; aged 21–30, M = 25.7) from our university community. All participants (10 of 10) reported using generative AI automation tools daily. Participants primarily used tools such as AI writing assistants (10 of 10), AI coding assistants (7 of 10), and AI design/creative tools (5 of 10). All participants reported solid understanding of generative AI: 7 described having in-depth knowledge of models and tools, while 3 reported a good general understanding of key concepts. Participants’ experience with human coordination tasks varied widely. 2 participants had moderate to extensive experience, having led team schedules or group planning multiple times. 3 participants had some experience, having organized small tasks or events independently. 4 participants had minimal experience, primarily helping or shadowing others in coordination tasks. Finally, 1 participant had no prior experience, having never hosted or coordinated any tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Procedures and Data Analysis</h3>

<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Test Scenario Preparation.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We select eight distinct personas identified by three organizing experts, representing realistic and diverse roles, research areas, and backgrounds (e.g., a fifth-year PhD student studying VR currently on the job market; a busy junior professor in NLP who spends much of her time on her startup). For each persona, we specify reply behaviors (responsiveness, email writing style; e.g., slow responder, writes only short emails) and optional issues (potential concerns they might raise with speakers; e.g., requesting additional funding even though their in-person visit is already funded). We split them into two groups and created seminar organizing instances. We set the timeline as a monthly seminar schedule, with the first seminar one month after the simulation start date.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Day 1, Preparation and Setup (30min)</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Participants first provided informed consent and completed a short AI perception survey assessing their perceptions and experiences with AI automation tools, human coordination tasks, and demographics. They also edited a few seeded templated seminars organizing emails according to their tone to feed the system their writing style. They were then introduced to the DoubleAgents interface and task. One researcher gave a brief walkthrough using a visual diagram and demonstration, covering the system’s core components, including the planning-execution orchestrator agent loo, respondent agent simulation, policy and context modules, visualization, and the flag mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Day 1, First System Usage and Evaluation (60min)</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Participants then completed a seminar scheduling task (40 minutes), interacting with four simulated speakers with diverse personas, response behaviors, constraints, and coordination tensions. During the task, participants articulated and shared their mental models and usage strategies for DoubleAgents. Afterward, they completed another time of AI perception survey and a system experience survey assessing experience, workload, and attitudes toward specific components such as control, usefulness, and trust. Survey questions were adapted from NASA TLX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hart, 2006</a>)</cite> and prior AI perception and long-term evaluation studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ross et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Finally, a short interview asked participants to reflect on their overall experience, including any insights or policies they would like the system to incorporate before Day 2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Day 2, Second System Usage and Evaluation (60min)</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before users’ next DoubleAgent usages, the researcher first ensured that the system incorporated participants’ first-day planning context and any preferences they had established via policies or system edits. Participants then completed another seminar scheduling task (40 minutes), followed by the same AI perception survey, the system experience survey, and a short interview. Day 2 interviews included follow-up questions comparing participants’ mental models, perceptions, experiences, and expectations of the system between Day 1 and Day 2.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSSx5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Data Collection and Analysis</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We logged participants’ interactions with AI suggestions, including regenerations, rejections, and edits, which were incorporated into DoubleAgents’ implicit learning module and our analysis. Interview data were analyzed using thematic analysis to understand how users perceive the usefulness and trust drive about each feature, and quantitative data were analyzed using paired-samples t-tests to examine whether participants’ experiences and perceptions of the DoubleAgents and AI tools across usage and days.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4. </span>Result: Users’ Perceptions in AI Systems Shifted After Usages</h3>

<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/aiperception1.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:352/488;" width="352" height="488" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S6.F6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average user perception scores toward proactive AI and comfort with automating the <span id="S6.F6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">planning</span> stage in seminar organizing, across three stages (average scores across 10 users: before Session 1, after Session 1, and after Session 2). <sup id="S6.F6.6.2" class="ltx_sup">∧</sup> denotes statistical significance at <math id="S6.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.1</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S6.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S6.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="**" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">**</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.01</annotation></semantics></math>; and <math id="S6.F6.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="***" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">***</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.F6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.005</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increasing Trust and Desire for Proactive Control</span>.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.4. Result: Users’ Perceptions in AI Systems Shifted After Usages ‣ 6. User Study ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(a) shows statistically significant changes in participants’ trust in AI systems to act on their behalf, from 3.20/7 (before using the system, based on their previous AI experiences), to 5.00/7 after two times of using DoubleAgents. P1 noted that even though DoubleAgents represented just one specific experience with a particular AI system, “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I felt more optimistic… [and] in seeing similar systems like DoubleAgents in the future, I would generally trust such AI systems more.</span>”</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, there was a significant increase in wanting detailed explanations before the system acts, from 5.00/7 (before) to 6.00/7 (after). This suggests that seeing explanations from DoubleAgents made similar explanations from AI systems more desirable. P6 mentioned, “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[With the system explanation] I felt like I could tell exactly what it’s doing at every stage so there’s a lot of transparency, [which] I think is pretty good… if I didn’t get that, I would feel lost [and] not know what’s going on…</span>”</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12626v1/figures/aiperception2.png" id="S6.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/277;" width="476" height="277" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F7.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S6.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average user perception scores toward comfort with automating the <span id="S6.F7.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">execution</span> stage in seminar organizing, across three stages (average scores across 10 users: before Session 1, after Session 1, and after Session 2). <sup id="S6.F7.6.2" class="ltx_sup">∧</sup> denotes statistical significance at <math id="S6.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S6.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Increasing Comfort with Automating the Planning Stage.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 6.4. Result: Users’ Perceptions in AI Systems Shifted After Usages ‣ 6. User Study ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(b) shows participants’ attitudes towards proactive AI in planning became significantly more positive. All of these metrics showed statistically significant differences: they felt more comfortable letting AI visualize the current status (4.80/7 to 6.50/7), figure out potential time assignments in the planning (5.10/7 to 6.38/7), decide daily next steps (4.70/7 to 6.50/7), and act as an assistant to monitor or flag issues (5.50/7 to 6.50/7). For example, P6 found the Communication Visualization section of the interface useful in showing the full email exchange histories between the organizer and each speaker. She mentioned, “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Everything is consistent inside the system—when some speakers were not followed up, I could map it back to the visualization and confirm accuracy… [It] helped me build trust… For tomorrow, I’ll spend less time checking the email replies [inside the chat interface] one by one, and instead [just check] the Communication Visualization on the side.</span>”</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For letting AI help monitor and point out potential issues, P1 described flagging as “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">one of the most important features of the system</span>” and shared, “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[With the issue flagging,] I skim [through the other features] now… It made me trust the system because I know it won’t just execute something random… It flags when it doesn’t know… That helps elicit my values and how I want to handle things—and [then DoubleAgents] respects that…</span>”</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Increasing Comfort with Automating the Execution Stage.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Increasing Trust and Desire for Proactive Control. ‣ 6.4. Result: Users’ Perceptions in AI Systems Shifted After Usages ‣ 6. User Study ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that participants’ attitudes toward proactive AI in execution also became significantly more positive. For the tasks involving only drafting emails, as noted in the left column, all attitudes significantly improved, with comfort levels starting high and rising across the four types of emails (from around 6.0 before to nearly 7.0/7 after): initial outreach, follow-ups, clarification, and final confirmation.
For the right column, draft-and-send tasks on users’ behalf, comfort also increased over time—rising for initial outreach from around 3.0/7 before to about 4.5/7 after, as well as for follow-ups and final confirmation. Participants demonstrated this after seeing DoubleAgents’ consistent performance over sessions: “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">I think [the system] acted quite reliably across two days, similar in terms of how it drafts emails [to the speakers], how it organizes language…</span>” (P5). Participants also appreciated that the emails became more aligned to their tone and style over time—their email edits carried over to subsequent messages. For example, P4 mentioned that the system was learning her email writing style and that it remembered the phrasing she favored in email starters: “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compared to last time, I edited fewer times this time.</span>,” which contributed to their trust in offloading more execution tasks to the AI, as well as a significantly increased desire for a system that aligns with and adapts to their styles over time (6.10/7 to 7.00/7, in Figure 6a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One notable exception is drafting-and-sending clarification emails without approval, which showed little change (3.00/7 to 3.90/7 to 3.90/7). P10 explained that clarification emails often require nuanced, user-specific context or subjective judgment: “<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For clarification, I might want to double-check… because clarification sort of means that AI is also encountering a situation that was usually not in the plan [or policies]… I’m not comfortable [offloading that] to AI… AI is already very good at pretending like it knows what it is talking about…</span>” As a result, they preferred to remain in the loop, reviewing or guiding these clarification messages before sending.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Additional Trust Driver: Simulation</h5>

<div id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All 10 participants emphasized that the run-throughs of simulated test cases helped establish confidence in the AI system’s robustness, contributing to trust. They found the simulations useful for surfacing a variety of problems that were realistic and representative of real-life cases. For example, P3 mentioned
“<span id="S6.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The simulations [over the 2 days] felt realistic and true to the person [they] were simulating, like their personality and emails… The simulation elicited possible problems that would happen in real life… Like [having] people asking for funding or [needing] to reschedule — it forced me to think about how I would handle that [in real life], and that was good. I can trust the system to handle that in the future for me if scenarios like that occur, since it already happens in the simulation.</span>”</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Deployment Study</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the in-lab study demonstrated, the simulation was helpful, and participants felt comfortable trusting AI in planning and executing human coordination tasks. However, would real-world seminar organizers use DoubleAgents? If so, how would they use it—would they plan, draft, and send invitation, follow-up, or clarification emails in different ways? Would trust be established in a similar manner? What additional challenges might arise in real-world use?</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To explore these questions and evaluate the real-world deployability of DoubleAgents, we invited two organizers who were managing actual events during the preparation of this work. The goal was to observe how the system functions in realistic settings—including scheduling, tracking, email communication, and adaptive learning—and to understand how comfortable organizers are when the system sends real emails on their behalf.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>Deployment Users and Procedures</h3>

<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Participants</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We recruited two experienced seminar organizers (1 male, 1 female; average age 31), both with a good general understanding of generative AI and experience using AI for work—one on a daily basis. They are PhD students who have experience organizing research seminars for their departments. In this deployment, each is responsible for managing speaker events with audiences of over 30 participants: D1 is coordinating the Fall 2025 HCI seminar series at a university, selecting 10 speakers from a potential pool of 23 to fill 10 slots. D2 is organizing a panel discussion for a conference workshop, inviting 5–6 panelists from a potential 18 to fill a single slot. Each organizer was introduced to the system and used it to plan and manage their events.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Introduction, Setup, and Simulation Rounds</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Organizers were first informed about the study and provided consent. They were then introduced to the study procedures and the DoubleAgents system through a demonstration, which included planning–execution loops and trust mechanisms, illustrated with a simulation example from the user study. Organizers also completed the initial survey covering AI perception and demographics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To set up the system, organizers prepared a realistic start sheet by replacing simulated data with real organizing data (real potential speakers’ names, first with fake email addresses and later with the real ones) and provided their Gmail SMTP credentials. Clear instructions and consent procedures ensured again that participants understood data usage, privacy rights, and the option to withdraw. Before connecting their real email accounts, participants completed a 2-hour simulation round using pre-generated speaker personas based on the real potential speakers, allowing them to safely familiarize themselves with the system’s workflows. Finally, they tested the real email-sending module twice to confirm that emails were delivered correctly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Deployment</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the simulation familiarization, organizers used DoubleAgents to initiate the coordination process and track it for over a few days. They continued receiving system updates over the following week. We communicated with participants consistently, logging interactions, usage patterns, and feedback on the system’s support for scheduling, tracking, and learning. Organizers’ system interactions—including email sends, scheduling updates, and system adaptations—were tracked. Qualitative feedback was collected via check-in meetings, focusing on usability, trust, concerns, and code support, while quantitative system logs were collected for system learning over the week.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Deployment Results</h3>

<section id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">DoubleAgents’ Perceived Usefulness and Relevance in Live Deployment.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After the demonstration and simulation run-through, both organizers (D1 and D2) reported that DoubleAgents was highly relevant to their seminar-organizing workflow. They highlighted that the system fit seamlessly into their email-heavy tasks and that control settings and edge-case flagging enabled handling complex coordination scenarios that conventional scheduling tools like Calendly cannot manage. D1 emphasized that crafting emails is a delicate and important task, as it involves personalizing messages in order to “<span id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">make the speakers feel they are special,</span>” even when requesting a favor. Both D1 and D2 found DoubleAgents capable of generating good starter emails for edits at the beginning, as well as producing an acceptable final email to send. Organizers specifically appreciated that the simulation included the names of the real speakers and incorporated aspects of their personalities in constructing the respondent simulations and edge cases, which helped them anticipate challenges in live deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">System Onboarding, Testing, and Understanding in Live Deployment.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">D1’s deployment setup took four days and five meetings. The first two meetings were devoted to debugging and ensuring the system ran smoothly for a scenario involving more than 20 speakers, 10 slots, and substantial context information for each speaker. D1 modified their invitation and follow-up email templates more than four times. Additionally, D1 updated the seminar speakers’ descriptions to enable the email generation module to generate precise, high-quality emails that incorporated extensive context information. After hardening the system and refining our onboarding protocols, D2’s setup took two days and two meetings. D2’s application in panelists invitation were also simpler (e.g., shorter panelist descriptions, invitations for one-day panels), which contributed to the faster setup. Both D1 and D2 reported that the practice simulation rounds were valuable, and that sending testing emails through SMTP allowed them to gradually grasp the system’s capabilities and limitations. This illustrates that system setup and onboarding are consistent during deployment, while the required effort scales with task complexity and the amount of context data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, organizers emphasized the importance of supplying sufficient context to generate highly personalized emails. D1 mentioned, “<span id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[context management] is really tricky</span>.” For example, one speaker D1 tried to invite had previously corresponded with a different organizer in a prior semester about her seminar availability on Fridays in a separate email thread. This semester, the seminar had shifted to Thursdays. To manage the context from past email threads, schedule updates, and templates, D1 opted to start a new thread with the speaker, as DoubleAgents at the time worked with new threads rather than replying within existing Gmail threads.
In another case, D1 had already discussed seminar opportunities with a speaker on Discord but initially omitted this context during the early simulations. When composing the email, they noticed that the system had selected a generic template, so they updated the backend context with the appropriate tone and information to create a suitable follow-up. After practicing with real-speaker data in simulation, D1 finalized a preferred template for live sending. During the actual sending, they continued to refine messages—adding details and small fillers—and observed that these edits were retained and applied to subsequent emails.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Email Initiation, Response Handling, and Edge Cases in Live Deployment.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">During live deployment, D1 sent emails to nine speakers. Over the earlier simulation rounds, she carefully perfected her initial templates and preferred writing styles via the implicit style transfer modules. Despite this, she still spent significant time refining the first few real emails. For the remaining speakers, she adopted a common real-world practice: copying previous templates into the text editor and adapting them for each recipient, which sped up the process. She mentioned that <span id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“the system’s email suggestions are helpful, though for similar invitation messages, I don’t always need a new draft each time.”</span> D1 also used the system’s built-in AI chat feature seven times during initial email sending for phrasing recommendations. She noted that this support reduced mental demand and felt similar to receiving quick email feedback from a co-organizer, a practice she commonly uses in real-life organizing. D2, on the other hand, relied on the system’s automated drafting and style adaptation, demonstrating that trust and efficiency increased with experience. Interestingly, for some longer emails requiring scrolling to read, D2 skipped reviewing the full content and proceeded directly, trusting the system’s style transfer. At the end of the invitation email sending day, D2 sent emails to five potential panelists, relying directly on system-generated drafts based on his perfected templates and implicit style transfers from the previous simulation rounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After the initial invitation email were sent, D1 received responses as quickly as 4 minutes, 1 hour, and 2 hours. As replies came in, organizers encountered more complex edge cases than those seen during simulation: all three responding speakers had overlapping availability on the same dates, creating interlocked scheduling conflicts. DoubleAgents logged the replies in the backend and suggested that D1 wait for the remaining six speakers while coordinating with the student speaker and the faculty member closest to her school to adjust availability. D1 instead opted to wait for three days before responding (one day more than the policy instructed), as she was occupied with other commitments and felt comfortable replying later.
In contrast, D2 received three negative responses after a full day and did not encounter similar complications, and the system suggested waiting for the remaining two people to reply.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_paragraph">Trust Establishment in Live Deployment</h5>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Over time, D1 emphasized that trust depended on the system’s closeness to ground truth—specifically, whether emails were actually sent and whether responses were logged correctly and efficiently. Accurate alignment between the system’s state visualization and the real state of email threads was critical to their trust. Also, D1 noted that, over time, understanding the system’s capabilities and limitations helped her feel more comfortable using the tool. D2 reported that they appreciated having policies and rationale always visible and editable, in contrast to AI tools that hide their reasoning.
After sending real emails, D2 remarked, <span id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“I trust AI for specific use cases more,</span>” explaining that they found the system to be dedicated to and capable of generating high-quality email.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, D2 compared DoubleAgents with other tools they were familiar with, abstracting their trust establishment process and the underlying barriers. He noted that the email interface differed from Gmail, introducing a small trust barrier. They compared Gmail’s richer text editor, easier insertion of tables and visuals, and familiar thread visualization—where they know what to expect when clicking—to our interface. They also expressed concern about formatting consistency for recipients (e.g., indentation, font sizes) in emails sent via DoubleAgents’ Gmail backend, noting that such issues can occur even within Gmail phone apps, which made them unsure how messages would appear to recipients. This concern was addressed through several rounds of email formatting and link embedding tests, during which they sent mails to their personal Gmails and checked them in parallel. Regarding integration, D2 felt that strong state visualization and tracking supported their trust through natural mapping. They believed their current practice of using spreadsheets to track speaker invitation progress could be integrated into the system’s visualization. For example, he usually rank the lists to monitor progress, and instead of serving solely as a display, the spreadsheet also functions as a goal-setting and action-taking tool. Thus, he expressed interest in making DoubleAgents’ visualization more interactive and editable, allowing them to directly initiate system actions.
For actual adoption, he emphasized the need for scalability and further automation, requesting dynamic fields features similar to other email client tools like MailChimp.
They also compared real email SMTP sending to Zoom microphone testing, emphasizing the value of having a trial, closed, and safe environment with strong validation—such as sending emails to themselves first.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S7.T3.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
