<span id="S7.T3.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:108.1pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Human-AI Design Challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2020</a>)</cite></span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S7.T3.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:159.3pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(1) Consistent and robust</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S7.T3.3.2.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Policy-based conditional reasoning grounded in human social cognition; reliable handling of varied test suites and edge cases</em></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Trust grows when others are </span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">reliable, predictable, and follow shared norms or values</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">. Consistency over time signals dependability. </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lansing et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rempel et al., 1985</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer et al., 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beratan, 2007</a><span id="S7.T3.3.2.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">)</span></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:102.4pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Consistency and standards</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Error prevention</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Help users recognize, diagnose, and recover from errors</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:108.1pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Unpredictability of AI behavior</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: users need repeated, consistent outputs to form expectations </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Data dependency</span><span id="S7.T3.3.2.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: robustness across edge cases builds confidence</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:159.3pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(2) Controllable and aligned with user values</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S7.T3.3.4.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">User can confirm and intervene at every step; system adapts to users’ editing history and evolving policies</em></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Trust strengthens when people </span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">respect autonomy</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, allow </span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">mutual control and negotiation</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, and </span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">align with shared goals or values</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lansing et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kramer, 1999</a><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.T3.3.4.2.1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:102.4pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- User control and freedom</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Flexibility and efficiency of use</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:108.1pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Conceptual misalignment</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: user oversight helps align system behavior with goals </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Evolving mental models</span><span id="S7.T3.3.4.4.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: adaptation supports shifting user practices</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:159.3pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">(3) Understandable and explainable</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S7.T3.3.6.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Real-time state tracking and visualization; clear reasoning and a chat window to ask questions</em></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:88.2pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Humans trust others who are </span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">transparent</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, </span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">communicate intentions</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">, and </span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">explain their actions clearly</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lansing et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kramer, 1999</a><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">)</span></cite><span id="S7.T3.3.6.2.1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:102.4pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Visibility of system status</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Help and documentation</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Match between system and the real world</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.3.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.7pt;padding-bottom:1.7pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:108.1pt;">
<span id="S7.T3.3.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.3.6.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">- Explainability gap</span><span id="S7.T3.3.6.4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">: reasoning and state tracking reduce opacity and support accurate mental models</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S7.T3.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S7.T3.19" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">How DoubleAgents’ trust-building dimensions map to human–human trust building literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rempel et al., 1985</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mayer et al., 1995</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kramer, 1999</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lansing et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beratan, 2007</a>)</cite>, Nielsen heuristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nielsen, 1992</a>)</cite>, and human-AI interaction design challenges <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Discussion: Trust Establishment with AI Agents Over Time, Consistency, Control, and Understanding</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DoubleAgents illustrates how users establish trust in a novel AI system over time. Users initially expressed low trust because they were unfamiliar with the system, but through exploration, testing, learning, and habituation, they gradually began to rely on it. This aligns with prior longitudinal studies on AI tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Long et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Understanding how this trust emerged sheds light on the mechanisms by which human–AI relationships can evolve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Trust Establishment in Live Deployment ‣ 7.2. Deployment Results ‣ 7. Deployment Study ‣ DoubleAgents: Exploring Mechanisms ofBuilding Trust with Proactive AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, DoubleAgents’ trust-establishment mechanisms center on three interdependent qualities: <span id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistency</span>, <span id="S8.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">control</span>, and <span id="S8.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">understanding</span>. These dimensions align with patterns of human–human trust development, are supported by classic usability heuristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nielsen, 1992</a>)</cite>, and address well-known challenges in human–AI interaction design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">First, one main design goal of DoubleAgents is to be <span id="S8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">consistent and robust</span>. The system employs policy-driven, conditional reasoning to stabilize its outputs and ground its behavior in shared human values and social norms. This design enables reliable performance even across diverse testing suites and edge cases, allowing users to form stable expectations—echoing how consistency signals dependability in human relationships and mitigating issues of unpredictability and data dependency in AI systems. Second, DoubleAgents incorporates mechanisms to remain <span id="S8.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">controllable and aligned with user values</span>. The system enforces stepwise confirmations and allows user intervention throughout planning and execution, while adaptively learning from users’ past editing histories and evolving policy additions. These mechanisms promote a sense of autonomy and mutual negotiation, reflecting how humans build trust through control-sharing, and addressing conceptual misalignments and evolving mental models in human–AI collaboration. Third, DoubleAgents is designed to be <span id="S8.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">understandable and explainable</span>. It offers real-time state tracking, clear visualizations, explicit reasoning for its actions, and a chat interface for user queries. These mechanisms increase system transparency and communicability, similar to how open communication fosters trust between humans, and reduce the explainability gap in AI systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Collectively, these design mechanisms enabled users to gradually transition from skepticism to reliance. By operationalizing consistency, control, and understanding, DoubleAgents supported the iterative habituation process through which users internalized the system’s behavior and incorporated it into their workflows. This suggests that trust in proactive AI systems is not a fixed property but an emergent outcome—shaped by interaction designs that allow users to explore, test, and progressively align the system with their own goals and values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Several participants compared building trust in DoubleAgents to their prior experiences with coding automation tools such as Cursor and CLine, reflecting the CS background of most participants. For example, P10 described first trying the tool on a safe project in a controlled environment (not pushing to a live website) to see how well it worked, illustrating the role of consistent system performance in building trust. They also highlighted that Cursor offered strong control and state-reverting capabilities, which allowed them to confirm each step and explore outcomes safely. Over time, they learned how to craft better prompts, demonstrating alignment with their goals and values. Finally, participants emphasized the importance of understandable reasoning: both Cursor and DoubleAgents provide stepwise explanations for each action, which helped them make sense of the system’s behavior and develop confidence in its outputs. As P4 reflected: <span id="S8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Most of the trust I had with Cursor was because it would ask at each step and then explain its reasoning for that step—and it was accurate. The same thing here [in DoubleAgents]: it’s accurate, and each step provides reasoning. It’s from the frequent confirmation and the reasoning always being correct that I built trust.”</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These accounts illustrate that consistent performance, controllable interactions, and clear explanations collectively scaffolded the sophisticated trust formation process between AI and users, aligning users’ experiences with the mechanisms designed into DoubleAgents and with broader theories of habituation and technology acceptance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Limitations and Future Works</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, DoubleAgents was initially evaluated through simulated seminar-organizing scenarios. While these simulations were reviewed by domain experts and deemed realistic, they may still lack the richness of real-world settings. Future work could make the ReAct-based coordination agent more user-aligned by incorporating users’ past email or organizing data and modeling their personal communication styles and values. Similarly, the respondent agent simulation could be made more realistic by drawing on a more diverse set of real human behavioral data.
Second, our system used different models for different tasks—o3 for reasoning tasks and 4o-mini for other tasks. Although our technical evaluation showed they performed well, future models with stronger social and human cognition capabilities, as well as incorporating users’ data, may achieve better planning and execution.
Third, although we deployed the system with two real organizers in real life, we also consulted others whose scenarios introduced additional complexities where the system may not perform perfectly. For example, one seminar organizer mentioned theirs involved bilingual invitations, and others described co-organizing dynamics, both of which introduce challenges not addressed in our current design.
Finally, all our participants were experienced users of AI tools. Their familiarity may have positively biased their trust and comfort levels. Future work should include participants with a broader range of AI experience to better understand adoption barriers among less experienced users.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present DoubleAgents, an agentic planning system that incorporates clear human intervention points, rich state visualizations, explicit policies, and uncertainty/edge<span id="S10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>case flagging to automate human coordination. In a two<span id="S10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>session lab study (n=10) and a week<span id="S10.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>long deployment with two organizers, participants reported significantly greater comfort delegating planning and execution to DoubleAgents. Simulated respondents provided a safe proving ground to observe policy<span id="S10.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>aligned planning and consistent system performance. From these studies, we distill three trust<span id="S10.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>by<span id="S10.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>design mechanisms—consistency, control, and understanding—operationalized via policy<span id="S10.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text">-</span>driven reasoning, stepwise approvals, implicit system adaptive learning, and a real-time system state. These patterns generalize beyond seminar scheduling to proactive agents design that must act while remaining auditable and aligned.</p>
</div>
</section>
